alf of last year  and and uh tasked with shepherding all  27 governments into making their  purchases together to have extra  leverage to make these big  procurements it also on the side  negotiated its own deal  with vaccine makers and that's something  that a lot of countries are are not  happy about germany of course  the wealthiest eu country a very  powerful country and at the time  the european council president so this  is something that the european uh that  the european commission  and the german government are being  asked about how was it really ethical  for germany to purchase  additional doses on this side uh ursula  van der leyen says that it did not break  the european  commission agreement that countries are  allowed to purchase additional doses  and that germany's extra vaccines will  come only after the commission shipment  is fulfilled  dw's terry schultz reporting from  brussels good to talk to you  deaths from the coronavirus have reached  a new one-day high here in germany  despite an ongoing nationwide lockdown  figures from the country's public health  agency show 1188 covet related deaths in  the past  24 hours and almost 32 000  new covet-19 cases were recorded that's  a little less than the previous peak in  mid-december but health officials warn  actual numbers could be higher  hans brown to our political  correspondent joins me now to talk some  more about this  hans how do we explain this new record  death toll  well the death figures are always  delayed by a couple of weeks after the  infection figures have peaked in other  words what we are seeing now are the  deaths  that are the result of the peaks in  infections that were recorded  mid-december  it always takes about a week or two  before people actually have symptoms  then it takes a while for them to go to  hospital  if they do go to hospital and then it  takes a while for them to die if they do  die so  the figures we see now are a factor of  the high infection rates that we had